Task 1:
-> Iterates through an array of numbers.
-> Ignores odd numbers.
-> Rotates each even number left until:
  -> It becomes 1, in which case it is ignored; or
  -> It has been rotated 32 times without becoming 1, in which case it is kept.
-> Copies only the retained numbers to another array.
-> Stores the number of copied values at the end.

Task 2:
- Subtask 1:
  -> Iterates through a list of 36-byte structures.
  -> Checks whether each date (day, month, year) is valid:
     - Year between 1990 and 2030;
     - Month between 1 and 12;
     - Day within that month's allowed range.
  -> Sets [esi+31] to 1 for a valid date and leaves it at 0 otherwise.
  -> Continues until all ecx records have been processed.
- Subtask 2:
  -> compare decides whether two structures, v[i] and v[j], should be swapped.
  -> Comparison order: valid events first, then chronological date, then lexicographical name for equal dates.
  -> It returns 1 when a swap is needed and 0 otherwise.
  -> swap exchanges two 36-byte structures one byte at a time.
  -> sort_events compares each pair (i, j) with j > i and calls swap when compare returns 1.

Task 3:
-> Processes the input byte string in groups of three bytes.
-> Converts each group into four ASCII characters representing the binary data.
-> Splits the 24 bits into four six-bit values.
-> Uses each value as an index in the alphabet table.
-> Writes four output characters, advances esi by three bytes and edi by four bytes, and repeats until the input is processed.

Task 4:
- check_row(char* sudoku, int row):
  -> Checks that the row contains digits from 1 to 9 without repetitions.
  -> Returns 1 for a valid row and 2 otherwise.
- check_column(char* sudoku, int column):
  -> Performs the same checks for a column.
  -> Returns 1 for a valid column and 2 otherwise.
- check_box(char* sudoku, int box):
  -> Finds the starting row and column of a 3x3 box and checks all nine cells.
  -> Returns 1 for a valid box and 2 otherwise.

Sorry if I slightly exceeded the 80-character line limit.